  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is **not** invertible\n- The lines $2x-3y=1$ and $2x-2y=2$ intersect at exactly one point\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false since, according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
